export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>The Anatomy of A Console</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>When game developers adopt a new console, the first thing they do is trying to draw a triangle. With the PS4, the average adoption time is 1 – 2 months. With the PS5, it is less than a month.</p>\n<p>To draw a triangle on a console, game developers must know how the different parts of the hardware coordinate. Specifically, they have to know how data is transferred from the storage to the GPU where graphics are generated. In this chapter, we’ll learn just that.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c7051cab2683dc7bc314.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c7051cab2683dc7bc314.webp\" width=\"560\" height=\"599\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d8f2bf7cc330e070bef4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d8f2bf7cc330e070bef4.webp\" width=\"598\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Binary &amp; data</strong></p>\n<p>The word bit comes from “binary digit.” Unlike the decimal system, which uses numbers between 0 – 9, the binary system only uses 0 and 1. Depending on the situation, these two numbers can represent on-and-off, yes-or-no, true-or-false, etc.</p>\n<p>By combining these two numbers in different ways, the computer can input and output any kind of data, from text to images and sounds.</p>\n<p>Since everything in computers is binary, you will see powers of two (8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, etc.) a lot. You will get why once we talk about texturing. J</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>A bit is either a 0 or 1. A byte consists of 8 bits.</p>\n<p>Bits are written with “b” while bytes are written with “B.” For example, 10 Kb = 10 kilobits; 10 KB = 10 kilobytes.</p>\n<p>Generally, bits are for measuring internet speed while bytes are for measuring the size of data. Because data can be very large, people add prefixes such as “kilo,” “mega,” and “giga” to bits and bytes.</p>\n<p>For example, downloading in a 10 megabytes (equal 80 megabits) at a 10-megabit rate will take 8 seconds.</p>\n<p>However, hardware-bound data transfer speeds are measured in bytes.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ba11336578f831fb55f9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ba11336578f831fb55f9.webp\" width=\"519\" height=\"600\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Hardware overview</strong></p>\n<p>Whether it is a console or a PC, a game device relies on these 4 components:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>CPU</strong> (Central Processing Unit): compile and send graphical data to the GPU</li><li><strong>GPU</strong> (Graphical Processing Unit): receive graphical data from the CPU and execute them</li><li><strong>RAM</strong> (Random-Access Memory): store temporary data to run in-use applications</li><li><strong>Storage</strong> (HDD or SSD): store permanent data; write and read data for the RAM</li></ul></td><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8379d450fb54b135763c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8379d450fb54b135763c.webp\" width=\"277\" height=\"267\" style=\"--source-width:127px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e0265ebad77d5e412387.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e0265ebad77d5e412387.webp\" width=\"264\" height=\"267\" style=\"--source-width:127px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>CPU</p></td><td><p>GPU</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b6c3bedb00913ef7a5bf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b6c3bedb00913ef7a5bf.webp\" width=\"277\" height=\"200\" style=\"--source-width:127px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/141c9fdee73789164dc8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/141c9fdee73789164dc8.webp\" width=\"264\" height=\"252\" style=\"--source-width:127px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>RAM</p></td><td><p>HDD</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a542937124e98f963aa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a542937124e98f963aa.webp\" width=\"423\" height=\"564\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>EXTRA – Motherboard</strong></p>\n<p>The motherboard connects all the individual components together and allows data to transfer between them.</p>\n<p>A motherboard also has many components of its own, but the ones that bring other pieces of hardware are the buses, which contain wires that connects everything together.</p>\n<p>Motherboard buses are similar to real buses: they transport data from one destination to another, from RAM to CPU or from CPU to GPU.</p>\n<p>Their speeds are measured in megahertz (MHz). If a bus is 200 MHz, it transfers 200 million data per second.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Storage</strong></p>\n<p>The storage stores permanent data of a game. <em>Permanent</em> means the data won’t be lost when you turn the console off.</p>\n<p>There are currently two storage types:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>HDD</strong>: more space, but slower transferring speed</li><li><strong>SSD</strong>: more expensive, but faster transferring speed</li></ul>\n<p>When you endure a long loading screen in a game, it’s either because there is too much data for the storage to load, or storage is too slow.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/97b492756124378099cb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/97b492756124378099cb.webp\" width=\"585\" height=\"597\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/93f34ddc155d9233f3be.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/93f34ddc155d9233f3be.webp\" width=\"583\" height=\"328\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Compare the ground textures.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6f598f9dc71eca74e03a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6f598f9dc71eca74e03a.webp\" width=\"632\" height=\"281\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>RAM</strong></p>\n<p>When you open a game, the storage has to transfer all data in that game to the RAM (Random Access Memory). Data has to be stored on RAM before it can be processed by the CPU.</p>\n<p>To illustrate the role of RAM in the rendering pipeline, we can turn to textures. More detailed textures occupy more space on the RAM. Therefore, a system with more RAM can load in more detailed textures. But whether or not those textures can be rendered is dependent on the GPU.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>EXTRA – VRAM</strong></p>\n<p>The normal RAM sends data to the CPU, VRAM sends data to the GPU. It stores all the graphics-related data for the GPU to access easier and quicker. VRAM is basically a frame buffer, preparing upcoming frames in advance for smoother rendering.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/15c4c6a0f1e980452b16.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/15c4c6a0f1e980452b16.webp\" width=\"502\" height=\"564\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4eac476e7a44ca50c940.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4eac476e7a44ca50c940.webp\" width=\"503\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>CPU &amp; GPU</strong></p>\n<p>The CPU is good at general tasks while the GPU is good at rendering graphics. That means, the GPU handles the visuals and leaves other things (such as physics and AI) to the CPU.</p>\n<p>However, the GPU cannot generate graphics itself. It needs to be guided by the CPU, which leads us to draw calls.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Draw call</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n<p>A draw call is basically a call from the CPU to the GPU to draw an object. This means the CPU has to works ahead of the GPU in order for the game to generate frames consistently. Therefore, when a game has a shaky frame rate, don’t just blame the GPU.</p>\n<p><sub><em>Keep in mind that the CPU also handles other things such as physics and AI, so CPU bottlenecking doesn’t necessarily equate to draw calls.</em></sub></p></td><td><div class=\"source-panel\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3833540c4d30d2a09ea9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3833540c4d30d2a09ea9.webp\" width=\"664\" height=\"206\" style=\"--source-width:127px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p><p>In this example, the CPU works 1 frame ahead of the GPU.</p><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9f1a81b88f84dd816c9a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9f1a81b88f84dd816c9a.webp\" width=\"663\" height=\"205\" style=\"--source-width:127px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p><p>Here the CPU doesn’t deliver draw calls fast enough, so the GPU stays idle.</p></div></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/caf241f4e102d82b17c8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/caf241f4e102d82b17c8.webp\" width=\"633\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Inside a draw call</strong></p>\n<p>Inside a draw call is information about textures, states, shaders, rendering objects, buffers, etc. The most important thing to remember is one draw call is required per material. Therefore, the more unique materials you have, the more draw calls are required, and that can drag the performance down.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/19f31ae4db66dbe18eb6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/19f31ae4db66dbe18eb6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"481\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/0a542937124e98f963aa.webp", "assets/library-inline/141c9fdee73789164dc8.webp", "assets/library-inline/15c4c6a0f1e980452b16.webp", "assets/library-inline/19f31ae4db66dbe18eb6.webp", "assets/library-inline/3833540c4d30d2a09ea9.webp", "assets/library-inline/4eac476e7a44ca50c940.webp", "assets/library-inline/6f598f9dc71eca74e03a.webp", "assets/library-inline/8379d450fb54b135763c.webp", "assets/library-inline/93f34ddc155d9233f3be.webp", "assets/library-inline/97b492756124378099cb.webp", "assets/library-inline/9f1a81b88f84dd816c9a.webp", "assets/library-inline/b6c3bedb00913ef7a5bf.webp", "assets/library-inline/ba11336578f831fb55f9.webp", "assets/library-inline/c7051cab2683dc7bc314.webp", "assets/library-inline/caf241f4e102d82b17c8.webp", "assets/library-inline/d8f2bf7cc330e070bef4.webp", "assets/library-inline/e0265ebad77d5e412387.webp"];
